00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.589 align:start position:0%
 
well let's talk about that with our next  guest the shadow home secretary nick  thomas simmons thank you very much for  being on the programme  thank you for listening across the  report that we just did too  um how concerned are you about the rise  in  children and young people being  exploited during the pandemic  it's a deep concern and just listening  then to alicia in that report  really did bring home to me firstly  the increasing number of county lines  things gone by  near 300 percent over the last decade  but also  during the pandemic as that report  brought out  it's clear that the profile of people  who have been drawn in are getting  younger  and more and more young women and girls  getting drawn in  as well now what we need to see it seems  to me are two major things  i think firstly we need to see strategic  leadership on this issue at the home  office i think it was a grave error  to abolish the serious violence task  force which was announced to great  fanfare  because often county lines are  associated with other types of crime  including gang violence  but secondly we need to be doing all we  can  to give these young people who are  getting drawn into county lines  the opportunity to make a very different  life and we see that for example  in london where the mayor of london has  introduced a  rescue and recovery fund to actually  invest to try and ensure  that people get the help they need this  is an awful crime it's exploiting young  people  and it really should be a top priority  now to try and do all we can to deal  with it  now staying on policy and crime you of  course are publishing labor's  a plan to tackle violent crime and help  support victims  what's the idea  well look firstly i think we have to  realize the scale  of the problem violent crime has gone up  in  every single police force area of  england and wales over the last  decade in four out of five areas it  has doubled and there are now 200  violent crimes happening every hour this  is a  chronic failure frankly under this  conservative government  violent criminals have never had it so  good and we've got  to tackle that so what do we need to do  and what am i suggesting  today well we need more police  out there on the streets not behind  desks  we need to make sure that we change our  approach to preventative services not  see them decimated as we have  over the last decade we need tougher  sentences for example  for rape and we need in my view now to  have a  victim's law victim should be at the  heart of our criminal  justice system and that's why i think we  actually need to put that  into law so there's a victim's law that  puts victims first  you know i've listened to what you said  and i sort of read into a bit of what  you're proposing as well and of course  the rise in violent crime is something  that everyone will be very concerned by  but i can't help thinking that some of  the ideas sound very good on paper but  actually things are a lot more  complicated than that i mean the first  thing you said for example is  insuring more police on the beat rather  than behind desks  but if violent crime cases have doubled  charging rates  have fallen down 22 percent in 2014-15  to 6.8  in 2019-20 it feels to me like the  problem  is actually convincing the crown  prosecution  service to take these cases forward it's  about building the case work i mean  that's often done from behind a desk  isn't it doesn't sound quite as  you know appealing as getting more  bobbies on the beat but that's a reality  isn't it  well no no it is a systemic failure and  the point that is made there about  charging rates is absolutely right as  well but that is  a failure right through the criminal  justice system from this government  which has  obviously cut the budget of the ministry  of justice by an extraordinary amount  since 2010 but my point is this you have  to have systemic  change right from the start so  we we took it took many years for the  government to admit that falling police  numbers were actually related to the  rise in violent crime and that's why now  we've got to focus on  visible policing community policing  which has suffered  so much over the past 11 years so that  that acts both as a  lincoln the eyes and ears of the local  community  but also assisting in terms of detecting  crime  but you're absolutely right to say that  this goes right through  the system and that's why i've suggested  the other things that i have  around crime prevention preventing  people from becoming victims of crime in  the first place  but then looking at it right through the  system and that's why  putting victims first is so so important  and that's why i believe  we now need to have legal duties to  ensure that victims get the support they  need  from start to finish in the criminal  justice system that doesn't just mean at  the end of the trial by the way because  just because a trial has ended  doesn't end the trauma for the victim we  also need to see a system whereby  victims are kept informed and have that  information support they need  post-trial as well talking about victims  you know the second  thing that you said you wanted to do was  tougher sentences for rape  the maximum sentence for rape is life  imprisonment and the problem  again is about getting those convictions  in the first place so in the year to  march 2020  1.4 of rape cases recorded by police  resulted in a suspect  being charged that's a real problem  tackling it isn't about getting more  bobbies on the beat it isn't about you  know easy wins on  getting parliament to vote through  tougher sentences  it's the difficult painstaking work in  getting  those convictions in the first place  isn't it  it absolutely is and i find this debate  so frustrating because  why are you just proposing these easy  wins then rather than  tackling the big problems  no i'm not we're not we've published a  uh  a support plan for rape survivors in  recent weeks in addition  to the measures that i've announced  today and you're absolutely right  because  99 of every 100 reported  rapes doesn't even reach legal  proceedings  let alone a conviction and what we need  to be putting in place  is that support for rape victims at the  very start secondly we need to be fast  tracking rape and serious sexual  violence cases through the system so  people aren't waiting  months and months in sometimes some  cases years for justice  but we also need and we've argued this  in recent weeks a specific minister with  responsibility for this  with responsibility for rape and serious  sexual violence for precisely the reason  sophie that you've just said so that you  can drive change  right through the system and that's  absolutely what we require in addition  to raising that minimum sentence for  rate which is currently at five years  but absolutely it is  changed through the system that we need  and i'm so frustrated  because i've asked for this for the last  five years and the situation is simply  getting worse okay i'm keen to  ask you about the protest that we've  seen this week outside at batley grammar  school this is after a teacher  showed an offensive cartoon of the  prophet muhammed the schools  apologized suspended the teacher who's  now under police protection  should teachers be allowed to show  cartoons of the prophet muhammad  well look in in this particular case i  think we just need  to lower tensions firstly  the school has actually taken action as  you've mentioned  in relation to this which i think was  the right thing to do but what we've now  got to do  moving forward is absolutely to be  putting  children learners first lowering those  tensions now  and actually moving forward i think that  is what is absolutely  essential now because to continue  to to raise tensions in this situation  certainly isn't going to assist anybody  at least the  least alone those people who absolutely  should be coming first who are those who  are learning  at that school in battley but you think  suspending the teacher was the right  thing to do  look i i think the school has acted that  has now  obviously taken place that action has  happened i think what now needs to  happen as i say  is we need to lower the tension and we  need now  to actually move on putting the children  first we can't have either  any situations where teachers or others  are somehow intimidated trying to go  about their work that is something  that's not acceptable what we have to do  is to move on now and ensure that the  children have that learning particularly  at the moment when you consider  all the disruption there's been to young  people's education over the past 12  months that absolutely has to come first  okay and we have been talking about  scotland a lot on the program after alex  salmond  set up this new party in scotland  which is going to be contesting the list  seats in the election  um ken mccaskill said it's all about  trying to build a super majority for  independence  we've heard from douglas ross the leader  of the scottish conservatives who says  he wants to effectively build a super  majority for the union labour is saying  no though why not  why not well because labour is putting  forward  its own strong arguments at this  election i mean what i see are the  nationalists splitting  obsessed with the constitutional  question the  conservatives simply seem to want to  play politics with the union it's the  labour party in scotland or  andhra sawah who is making absolutely  clear  the focus now has to be on the recovery  from the coronavirus i think quite  rightly that's what people  not just in scotland but people right  across the united kingdom  should expect of political parties we've  had this huge national crisis  with the coronavirus now is the point to  actually get that recovery  plan in place anasawa has spoken very  powerfully about that  whether it's around education and the  catch-up that's required whether it's  around our national health service  and the nhs in scotland and all the work  that needs to be done  on non-coronavirus cases our community  recovery  that's the case that we are putting  forward  to the people of scotland anas is making  that case very powerfully  and of course i will support them in any  way i can in doing that  and just quickly uncovered we also heard  from oliver dowd and of course the  culture secretary and we were discussing  the idea of this  traffic light system for foreign travel  and amber light for example meaning you  just have to quarantine for a short  period with enhanced testing  is that something the labour would  support  well look if that is put forward in due  course i'll of course look at it and  consider it in the light of the  circumstances of the time  but the priority at the moment is the  gaping hole in our defenses  at the border and closing that we have a  situation where we've got  vaccine rollout and great credit to  those of the front line doing that  and the sacrifices the british people  have made over the past 12 months  and yet we do not have that  comprehensive  hotel quarantining system in place at  our borders i think only about one  percent of arrivals at the moment  are actually being put in hotel  quarantine now that has to change i've  argued that for  weeks and weeks and the reason i argue  it  is because we do not want to see a  variant of the  virus reaching our shores that threatens  the efficacy of our vaccines  we have to do everything we possibly can  to stop that  and that should be the priority at the  moment in the form of a hotel  uh quarantining system that's  comprehensive  just finally reports on the papers today  that annelise dodds  could lose her position as shadow  chancellor and be sacked in a reshuffle  are you a little bit relieved you've  been put up for the morning round this  morning perhaps  saying or might be safe in your position  i don't take any notice of the gossip  and the speculation that appears in the  newspapers around this it's something  that  part of political life anna lisa dodds  i've been working with over the past uh  12 months  is doing an excellent job and as a very  strong team  led by kiyosama we are we are the ones  who are putting forward the very clear  choice of this election  of a labour party that's on the side of  communities recovering from the  coronavirus  around the country in contrast to i'm  afraid a conservative party that just  wants to take this country back to the  past  a politician not interested in reshuffle  speculation that must be a first  nick thomas simmons thank you very much  for being on the program

